    "explanation": "return sends a value back from a function to the code that called it. Then you can save it, print it, or use it in math:\n\ndef double(n):\n    return n * 2\n\nresult = double(4)   # result is 8\n\nA function stops as soon as it reaches return.",
    "why": "print shows a value on the screen, but the rest of your program can't use it. return hands the value back, like a vending machine handing you a snack. Where the call was, Python puts the returned value.",
    "exampleCode": "def double(n):\n    return n * 2\n\nresult = double(4)\nprint(result)\nprint(double(10) + 1)",
