      "meaning": "Try some code, and if a certain error happens, run a backup plan.",
      "like": "a safety net under a trapeze."
     }
    ],
    "explanation": "Some errors you can see coming. If someone types \"abc\" when you asked for a number, int() raises a ValueError.\n\ntry and except let you catch that error and do something friendly instead of crashing:\n\ntry:\n    n = int(text)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"That's not a number\")",
    "why": "Python runs the try block. If an error happens inside it, Python jumps straight to the matching except block instead of stopping the program. If no error happens, the except block is skipped.",
    "exampleCode": "text = \"abc\"\ntry:\n    n = int(text)\n    print(\"Double is\", n * 2)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"That is not a number\")",
    "exampleNote": "int(\"abc\") fails, so the except part runs.",
    "walkthrough": [
