    "id": "try-safe",
    "title": "A safe number function",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "xp": 13,
    "terms": [],
    "explanation": "You can put try and except inside a function, so the rest of your program never has to worry about bad input.\n\nA function like safe_int(text) gives back a number if it can, and a backup value if it can't.",
    "why": "The return inside try only happens if int() worked. If it failed, Python jumps to except, which returns the backup. Either way the caller always gets a number back.",
    "exampleCode": "def safe_int(text):\n    try:\n        return int(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        return 0\n\nprint(safe_int(\"42\"))\nprint(safe_int(\"oops\"))",
    "exampleNote": "Good text gives its number. Bad text gives 0.",
    "walkthrough": [
     {
      "code": "return int(text)",
      "note": "If this works, the function returns the number."
     },
     {
      "code": "except ValueError:",
      "note": "If it fails..."
     },
     {
      "code": "return 0",
      "note": "...return 0 instead."
     },
     {
      "code": "print(safe_int(\"oops\"))",
      "note": "Prints 0, no crash."
     }
    ],
